# What anyone who can open the board can do and see

This is the complete list for `workbook serve` as of branch
`fix/board-multi-user-review`, route by route, ready for the hosting guide. The
same list, in prose, is committed to `docs/reference.md` under "What anyone who
can open the board can do and see".

The board has no roles. Whoever can open it — on a person's own machine, that
person; behind a sign-in proxy, everyone the proxy lets in — can do everything
below, through the page or by calling the routes directly.

Every write is recorded under the serving checkout's `user.email`, whoever made
it. The board cannot tell people apart, so nothing is limited to the person who
wrote it first, except withdrawing assignments, which core limits to the
assignee or whoever recorded the assignment. Every write is published to
`origin` like any other change: inline, or handed to a sync watcher.

## Read

| Route | What it gives |
| --- | --- |
| `GET /` (`?deleted=1`, `?q=`, `?priority=`, `?label=`, `?key=`) | The board: every active task's ID, title, priority, labels and assignees; descriptions on request; deleted tasks with `?deleted=1`. |
| `GET /tasks/new`, `GET /tasks/<id>` | The new-task form, and a task's full page: description, comments with their authors, attachments with who added them and when, assignments with who holds and who made each, dependencies, history. |
| `GET /config` | The configuration page: statuses, priorities, keys, project name and colors. `404` on a board built without the status mutations. |
| `GET /api/tasks` (`?deleted=true`, `?deleted=include`) | Every task in full, active and/or deleted, as JSON, including comments, attachment metadata and assignments. |
| `GET /api/tasks/<id>/history` | Every recorded change to the task: the recording identity's email, wall time, and old and new values. Text since edited or removed — an old title, a comment's earlier body — stays readable here. |
| `GET /api/tasks/<id>/attachments/<aid>` | The bytes of any file attachment still live on that task. Images (`gif`, `jpeg`, `png`, `webp`) inline; everything else as a download. Removed attachments answer `404`; compacted ones `410`. |
| `GET /api/vocabulary` | Statuses with labels, tags and forwarding; priorities with labels, tags and colors; keys and which is current; project name and colors; the configuration head. |
| `GET /api/sync` | Publication mode, whether a watcher answers, and `no-origin` / `no-watcher`. Not `origin`'s address. |
| `GET /healthz` | That the board is up. |

## Write

| Route | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `POST /api/tasks` | Create a task with any title, description, status, priority, labels and key. |
| `PATCH /api/tasks/<id>` | Change any task's title, description, status, priority, labels. |
| `PATCH /api/tasks/<id>/status`, `PATCH /api/tasks/<id>/position` | Move any task between columns and within one. |
| `DELETE /api/tasks/<id>` | Delete any task. This records a tombstone; nothing is erased. |
| `POST /api/tasks/<id>/restore` | Restore any deleted task, into any status and position. |
| `PUT` / `DELETE /api/tasks/<id>/dependencies/<dep>` | Add or remove any prerequisite. |
| `POST /api/tasks/<id>/comments` | Comment on any task. |
| `PATCH /api/tasks/<id>/comments/<cid>` | Rewrite anyone's comment. The comment keeps its original author and is marked edited; the history records the edit under the server's identity. |
| `DELETE /api/tasks/<id>/comments/<cid>` | Remove anyone's comment. The body stays in history. |
| `POST /api/tasks/<id>/attachments` | Attach a file (up to 1 MiB; 10 MiB live and 50 attachments per task) or an http/https link to any task. A link whose address holds a control or bidi-control character is refused. |
| `DELETE /api/tasks/<id>/attachments/<aid>` | Remove anyone's attachment. The bytes stay in history and on disk. |
| `POST /api/tasks/<id>/assignments` | Assign anyone (any email address, optionally `/label`) to any task; an empty value assigns the server's identity. Always additive. |
| `DELETE /api/tasks/<id>/assignments` | Withdraw an assignment that names the server's identity or was recorded by it. Others are refused. |
| `POST /api/vocabulary/statuses`; `PATCH` / `DELETE /api/vocabulary/statuses/<s>`; `PUT /api/vocabulary/order` | Add, rename, relabel, retag, remove and reorder statuses. Removal moves the status's tasks where the request says. |
| `POST /api/vocabulary/priorities`; `PATCH` / `DELETE /api/vocabulary/priorities/<p>`; `PATCH .../<p>/position`, `.../<p>/default`, `.../<p>/color` | The same for priorities, plus the default priority and each priority's color. |
| `POST /api/vocabulary/keys`; `PATCH /api/vocabulary/keys/<k>` | Add a key, and make any key current, retired or active again. This changes the prefix every new task's ID is minted under. |
| `PATCH /api/display` | Rename and recolor the project. |
| `PUT /api/sync` | Switch the server between publishing inline and handing changes to a watcher. This is server-wide: it changes what happens to everyone's next change. |

Limits that hold for everyone:

- 1 MiB per request body (about 1.4 MiB for an upload).
- The task field ceilings in `docs/reference.md`.
- 1 MiB per file, 10 MiB of live files and 50 attachments per task.
- 500 comments per task.
- 10 s to send headers, 30 s to send a request, 2 min idle.

Limits that do not exist yet:

- No limit on the number of tasks.
- No limit on the total bytes one person writes. Removal frees nothing, because
  history is append-only.
- No limit on how many requests one person has in flight.

## What the board reveals about the machine serving it

| Detail | Revealed today? | Where |
| --- | --- | --- |
| The server's folder | **Partly.** The last path element always, the full path sometimes. | The page header reads `Repository: <last path element>`. A failed configuration publication or an operational error can carry a full server path inside Git's message, for example `fatal: '/srv/…/repo.git' does not appear to be a git repository`. |
| `origin`'s address | **Yes, when publishing fails.** No route reports it otherwise. | A change whose publication fails — a task created or edited, or a status, priority, key or display change — is answered `200` with an `auto-sync-incomplete` warning carrying the failing Git command, its exit status and Git's message, which names `origin`'s full URL including its path, or the server's path to `origin` when that is a folder. Seen with `origin` at an unreachable address, from `POST /api/tasks`: `… publishing it failed: cannot ask origin which Workbook project it holds before publishing: git ls-remote --refs origin refs/workbook/project failed: exit status 128: fatal: unable to access 'http://127.0.0.1:1/team/secret-path/repo.git/': Failed to connect …`. |
| `origin`'s credential | **The user name and password in a remote's address: no.** Fixed by this pull request; before it, yes. **Anything else: possibly.** | Before the fix, the user name and password of `origin`'s address appeared in the warning when the remote answered with something other than Git ("no DAV locking support on http://user:token@…"), and in Git's own messages whenever the password held a slash. When a Git command fails, Workbook now reads the addresses Git is configured with (`remote.*.url`, `remote.*.pushurl`, `url.*.insteadOf`, `url.*.pushInsteadOf`, and the addresses those rewrites produce) and removes each one's credential, by its exact text, from Git's error output and from the command an error names. That lookup happens only when the failing command's error output contains an `@`. A password of eight characters or more is also removed on its own wherever it appears; a user name of that length is removed on its own only when its address has no password (a token is often the whole userinfo), so a user named `workbook` beside a password leaves `refs/workbook/…` and a path `/workbook/` as written. **Not removed:** whatever a credential helper, a hook or the remote itself prints (a helper writing `refreshing token <token>` to its error output is reported as written), and a token placed in an address's path or query string (`?access_token=…`). A deployment should give the server its credential by a means that keeps it out of the address: a credential helper that prints nothing, or an SSH key. |
| The server's Git identity | **Yes.** | The serving checkout's `user.email` is in the page (`data-assign-identity`, the assignment control), on every change the board records, and in history. |
| Teammates' identities | **Yes.** | Every author, assignee, assignment creator and history actor's email address. |
| Command output from the server | **Yes, on failure.** | Operational errors name the failing Git command and include Git's stderr; every publication warning, for a task change as for a configuration change, names the failing Git command and includes Git's stderr. |
